      {
        "id": 225984,
        "name": "Jimeni",
        "username": "Jimeni",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/j/77aa72/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2019-03-14T06:56:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>1. I don't recall if it's specifically mentioned in the starter kit ruleset, but if you look at the various pregenerated character sheets, they all have principles that, when used, you take the Max die. It's not exactly a rule, it's more of a character ability. But all character's have a form of it.</p>\n<p>2. Yeah, it means use the Min die of the player's roll. You get twists from a player's overcome check where they will succeed with a minor (or major) twist. So whatever they rolled for that overcome check, in the examples you mentioned, use the Min die that was just rolled. So it's probably a good idea to let your players know that whenever they roll their dice pool, they shouldn't pick up the dice too quickly because you may need another result from it.</p>\n<p>Good luck and have fun!</p>",
        "post_number": 2,
        "post_type": 1,
